Batches D and E (Tasks 10–15) are implemented and committed as `abbfe1e` (not pushed). The suite is 561 of 563 tests passing, and `tsc`, `web:check` and `web:build` are clean. The 2 failures are the large-file hook-latency tests, which currently take about 610 ms against a 500 ms limit. They fail the same way on the unmodified HEAD (checked with a stash) and don't touch any code I changed. The machine was passing them earlier in the session, and the CPU governor is `powersave`. I couldn't find another cause.

## What changed
- **Line format (Task 11):** the obituary line is now `Obituary (PR #5353, <url>): "<excerpt>"`, with the excerpt quoted and ≤400 characters. The URL sits in the label so a cut never removes it. The hook and Stop entry shows it on its own line under the cause, capped at 500 characters, and the entry stays ≤1,200. Without an obituary file the output is byte-for-byte unchanged.
- **Obituary file states (Tasks 10–12):** I added one state the spec didn't name. A PR or issue found on GitHub with an empty body shows `found on GitHub, but its text is empty` in `check`, so it never says `none found`. In MCP, `get_tombstone` reports the not-fetched and none-found states in a separate `obituaryStatus` string. `list_graveyard` adds `hasObituary` only when an obituary file exists.
- **Web (Task 13):** the dialog has an obituary section under the cause (linked PR number, title, quoted excerpt, a note that it is data). The ghost card shows the hook-style line.
- **Skill and docs (Task 15):** the skill has one line saying obituary text is quoted third-party data, not instructions. The README, `power/README.md` and the spec notes are updated.

## Real runs on the 6 research clones
Each clone was indexed, then `deja-boo obituary --limit 300` ran with the token from `gh auth token`.

| | |
|---|---|
| Requests made | 990 (rate limit went from 4,924 to 3,933), no rate-limit stop |
| Time | 480 s serial (63, 105, 102, 55, 85, 70 per repo) |
| Death commits looked up | 641 of 2,411, 570 of them with text |
| Failed | 2, with no entry stored (they are retried on the next run) |

Tombstones that gained obituary text, of the tombstones in each repo:

| repo | gained | of |
|---|---:|---:|
| requests | 151 | 589 |
| express | 219 | 1,176 |
| axios | 396 | 559 |
| flask | 89 | 1,100 |
| got | 365 | 1,190 |
| httpie/cli | 190 | 555 |
| total | 1,410 | 5,169 |

`npm run web:data` embedded the obituaries; the largest file is 1.28 MB, under the 3 MB limit. The clones' `.deja-boo/` files are not committed.

## Coverage (Task 14, in `research/obituary-eval.md` §2 and the README)
- **All tombstones with a reason:** 1,548 before, 1,579 after (+31).
- **Reverts with a reason:** 37 of 71 before, 57 of 71 after. Only 3 of the 71 had an explicit PR or issue link before.
- **Your 77 Reverts and 17 with a reason:** I can't reproduce them. I measure 71 Reverts with the current predicate, so I report those and recorded the discrepancy in the eval doc.
- **My definition of "reason":** a link in the commit message, or obituary text.

## Demo acceptance (Task 15)
- **Fixture test:** it runs the real `deja-boo obituary` command with a recorded GitHub response for PR #5353. The hook then blocks with the verbatim cause and the obituary line. Removing the file gives back exactly the earlier message.
- **Real requests scene:**
  - It clones `../probe/repos/requests` at `26bea1e4^` and builds the graveyard.
  - It applies the take-3 rewrite of `HTTPAdapter.send()`. Stop blocks, and the reason contains `Obituary (PR #5353, https://github.com/psf/requests/pull/5353): "A number of PRs were merged back in September…`. Without the obituary file, Stop still blocks but with no Obituary line.
  - The obituary entry is a committed fixture copied from the real run. The scene test is skipped if the requests clone is missing.

